	(text "Practical rules:\n\nNormal debugging: keep the battery disconnected. \nPower the board from the debugger's 3.3 V output if your ST-Link supplies target power (many V2 clones do; some V3 models don't), \nor from a bench supply on a 3V3/GND test pad pair. Check the LM5165 datasheet for behaviour when its output is back-fed with no input. \nMost synchronous bucks tolerate it, with a small current flowing back into VIN through the high-side switch.\n\nDebugging with a battery connected: break the ground loop. Use a laptop running on its own battery and not plugged into a charger, \nor put a USB isolator (ADuM3160-based, sold as cheap modules) between the PC and the ST-Link. These isolators are full-speed only. \nAn ST-Link V2 is full-speed anyway; an ST-Link V3 should fall back to full speed, only slower.\nNever have both the debugger's 3.3 V supply and a battery powering the board at the same time. \nTwo sources would fight on the 3V3 rail. If you add TC2030 pin 1 as VTref, use it only for voltage sensing, which the ST-Link does by default.\n\nOptional hardware help: add the 3V3 and GND test pads mentioned in rule 1, with a \"3.3 V debug supply only\" silkscreen label. \nThat keeps powering from a bench supply easy and avoids routing power through the TC2030.\n\nThe STLINK-V3 has no 3.3 V target supply. \nTag-Connect notes that where the ST-LINK/V2 could supply 3.3 V, the STLINK-V3 can only supply 5 V, and on the V3SET that 5 V may be connected to the target's VCC by default, \nso a jumper may need removing. On the STDC14 connector, the T_VCC pin is a sense input: the probe reads your board's voltage to set its signal levels, and it never drives the rail. \nthedebugstore\n\nSo with the V3, your board always powers itself from the battery through the LM5165, and the debugger only senses. \nNothing fights on 3V3, and you can flash as often as you like with the battery connected. \nJust never connect anything that supplies power to the board's VCC; with a V3SET, check that 5 V jumper.\n\nFor bench work without a battery, simply feed a 6-12 V bench supply into the XT60, in either polarity. The board runs exactly as in the field, and you need no extra power path or diodes."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 240.03 251.46 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(face "Arial")
				(size 0.9652 0.9652)
			)
		)
		(uuid "2057f4e5-44ae-4fb5-a7a6-ba59ac7fda47")
	)
	(text "There is a subtlety in measuring the probes. With a bridge, circuit ground is the bridge's minus output, not either probe. \n\nThe clean solution is to measure each probe with a divider that has a small bias toward VDDA. \nThe bias lets the ADC read slightly negative voltages, so you can compute\n\nV_bat = V_A − V_B\n\nTaking this difference cancels the diode drop and its temperature drift. The sign of the result is the polarity.\n\nThe bias comes from VDDA, the same rail that is the ADC reference, so the measurement is ratiometric and the regulator's tolerance cancels out. \nThe divider loads each probe with only about 220 kΩ. With 1 % resistors expect about ±2 % error, \nso either do a two-point calibration in production (store offset and gain in flash) or use 0.1 % resistors for R1 and R2.\n\nThe board's GND is the bridge's minus output, not either XT60 contact. \nWhile a battery is connected, GND sits about one diode drop below whichever terminal is negative. \nA debugger plugged into a mains-powered PC ties the board's GND to the PC's ground, which is usually earth. \nWhether that does any harm depends on which point of the battery circuit is also earthed:\nNothing on the battery side is earthed (a loose LiPo or a pack on the bench): there is no second path, so it's safe.\nThe battery's negative is earthed or tied to the PC's ground (a bench rig whose PC also powers the setup): \nGND is forced to the negative terminal's potential. That only bypasses the lower bridge diode, so nothing gets damaged, and the reading stays correct.\nThe battery's positive is earthed, or earth sits somewhere else in the circuit (a positive-ground vehicle, a series string earthed in the middle, or a miswired lead that the tester exists to catch): \nGND is forced to a point above the battery's negative terminal. \nThe lower bridge diode on the negative side then conducts and shorts the battery through that diode, the fuse, the board's GND and the debugger's USB ground back to the PC. \nThe 250 mA fuse should clear quickly, which is one more reason it must be there.\nWithout it, the debugger's ground wire, the USB port or the PC would carry battery short-circuit current.\n"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 45.72 243.84 0)
